alse,"party":"R","sponsorshipDate":"2025-12-18","sponsorshipWithdrawnDate":null,"state":"MN"},{"bioguideId":"S001213","fullName":"Rep. Steil, Bryan [R-WI-1]","isOriginalCosponsor":false,"party":"R","sponsorshipDate":"2025-12-18","sponsorshipWithdrawnDate":null,"state":"WI"},{"bioguideId":"H001100","fullName":"Rep. Hurd, Jeff [R-CO-3]","isOriginalCosponsor":false,"party":"R","sponsorshipDate":"2026-01-15","sponsorshipWithdrawnDate":null,"state":"CO"},{"bioguideId":"M001228","fullName":"Rep. Maloy, Celeste [R-UT-2]","isOriginalCosponsor":false,"party":"R","sponsorshipDate":"2026-01-21","sponsorshipWithdrawnDate":null,"state":"UT"},{"bioguideId":"A000370","fullName":"Rep. Adams, Alma S. [D-NC-12]","isOriginalCosponsor":false,"party":"D","sponsorshipDate":"2026-01-22","sponsorshipWithdrawnDate":null,"state":"NC"},{"bioguideId":"F000459","fullName":"Rep. Fleischmann, Charles J. \"Chuck\" [R-TN-3]","isOriginalCosponsor":false,"party":"R","sponsorshipDate":"2026-01-30","sponsorshipWithdrawnDate":null,"state":"TN"},{"bioguideId":"M001196","fullName":"Rep. Moulton, Seth [D-MA-6]","isOriginalCosponsor":false,"party":"D","sponsorshipDate":"2026-06-29","sponsorshipWithdrawnDate":null,"state":"MA"},{"bioguideId":"D000594","fullName":"Rep. De La Cruz, Monica [R-TX-15]","isOriginalCosponsor":false,"party":"R","sponsorshipDate":"2026-08-03","sponsorshipWithdrawnDate":null,"state":"TX"}],"fetchedAt":"2026-09-16T20:50:48+00:00","id":"hr2672-119","introducedDate":"2025-04-07","latestAction":{"actionDate":"2025-04-07","text":"Referred to the House Committee on the Judiciary."},"laws":[],"number":"2672","originChamber":"House","policyArea":"Immigration","sponsors":[{"bioguideId":"C001126","district":15,"fullName":"Rep. Carey, Mike [R-OH-15]","party":"R","state":"OH"}],"summaries":[{"actionDate":"2025-04-07","actionDesc":"Introduced in House","text":"<p><strong>Religious Workforce Protection Act</strong></p><p>This bill allows the Department of Homeland Security (DHS) to extend the nonimmigrant visa status of certain religious workers.</p><p>Under current law, if specified conditions are met, nonimmigrant religious workers may receive a visa for a period not to exceed five years. The bill allows&nbsp;DHS to grant an extension until the individual’s application for adjustment of status to permanent resident or an immigrant visa has been processed and a decision has been made. To be eligible for the extension, the individual must be (1) the beneficiary of a certain type of immigrant petition, and (2) eligible for such immigrant status&nbsp;absent the application of certain numerical limitations.</p><p>Such individuals who have pending adjustment of status applications are also granted certain job&nbsp;flexibilities, such as the ability to change employers. Individuals who have previously departed the U.S. due to the expiration of their visa are exempt from the one-year foreign residence requirement to renew their visa.</p>","updateDate":"2026-02-19T19:41:21Z","versionCode":"00"}],"title":"Religious Workforce Protection Act","type":"hr","updateDate":"2026-08-04T08:07:31Z","updateDateIncludingText":"2026-08-04T08:07:31Z"}
